@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: EXPERIMENT — can an external append-only ledger coordinate an agent COUNCIL of several `proveo run` instances?
' status: proposed 2026-09-10, not implemented; nothing in src depends on this file
' would touch: internal/sbx/run.go (Running, RunningNames), internal/run/helpers.go (StateDir)
' would touch: internal/egress/plan.go (host.docker.internal add-host gating)
' would touch: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (permissions.network.allow)
'   who signs an entry ........... _spec/_experiments/operator-fingerprint.puml
'   why each run is isolated ..... _spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml
'   the host-wide-store hazard ... _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the reachability switch ...... _spec/_plans/claude-in-chrome-reachability.puml
'   the evidence a run already has _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── AUTHORITATIVE SOURCES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   the candidate : https://github.com/cloudllm-ai/mentisdb
'   sandbox ports : https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/sbx/ports/
'
' ── THE QUESTION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Several `proveo run` instances working at once — a COUNCIL rather than one
' agent — need somewhere to see each other. Today they cannot: each run is a
' separate sandbox with its own sid, its own clone, its own evidence directory,
' and NOTHING that tells run B what run A has claimed or concluded.
'
' proveo can already enumerate them — `sbx.RunningNames()` — so the missing
' piece is not discovery. It is a shared, durable place to write.
'
' ── THE CANDIDATE, IN ITS OWN WORDS ─────────────────────────────────────────
' MentisDB describes itself as a "durable semantic memory engine and versioned
' skill registry for AI agents — a persistent, hash-chained brain that survives
' context resets, model swaps, and team turnover." Rust daemon with REST/MCP/
' HTTP, a library crate, and a Python client; MIT; 0.10.x at ~750 commits.
'
' WHAT MAPS ONTO WORK THIS TREE HAS ALREADY DONE, which is more than expected:
'   append-only, SHA-256 hash-chained   the tamper-evidence chain
'                                       operator-fingerprint.puml specified by
'                                       hand for the attestation ledger
'   an AGENT REGISTRY carrying          the identity surface that file needs, in
'   per-chain SIGNING KEYS (Ed25519)    a store that already expects one
'   `as_of` point-in-time queries       audit replay: what did the council
'                                       believe at time T
'   typed relations — Supersedes,       a correction is a first-class edge
'   Corrects, ContinuesFrom             rather than a rewritten row
'   an MCP interface                    a reach an agent already knows how to use
'
' So the shape is right, and that is exactly why the failure modes below need
' stating before anyone adopts it.
'
' ── IT DOES NOT SOLVE THE WALL, AND THE WALL IS THE SAME ONE ───────────────
' A hash chain proves that an entry was not ALTERED OR REORDERED after it was
' written. It says nothing about whether the entry was TRUE when written. If the
' agent holds the signing key and writes its own entries — which is the whole
' design — then the council's ledger is a tamper-evident record of what the
' agents CLAIMED, and nothing more.
'
' That is the identical distinction operator-fingerprint.puml reaches: a
' signature by the subject over evidence about the subject authenticates origin,
' not truth. The chain is still worth having; it closes the "edited afterwards"
' half that a transcript in a writable mount cannot close today. It must simply
' not be sold as honesty.
'
' ── THE LOAD-BEARING UNKNOWN: AN APPEND-ONLY LOG IS NOT A LOCK ─────────────
' The council use case is not memory, it is CLAIMING. Run A takes a task; run B
' must not take the same one. An append-only log cannot decide that by itself —
' two runs can each append "I claim X" and both entries are perfectly valid,
' perfectly signed and perfectly chained.
'
' So the question that decides whether MentisDB serves the COUNCIL, as opposed
' to serving MEMORY, is whether it offers a linearisation primitive: a
' compare-and-set, a lease, a unique constraint, an append-if-head-unchanged.
' UNKNOWN from the README. If it does not, the council needs one elsewhere and
' the ledger is an audit trail rather than a coordinator — still useful, but a
' different claim.
'
' MEASURE THAT FIRST. Everything else here is downstream of it.
'
' ── AND CLONE MODE MEANS IT COORDINATES INTENT, NOT MERGES ─────────────────
' Each run works in its own clone (clone-workspace.puml), so two runs cannot
' corrupt one working tree — but their COMMITS diverge and still have to be
' fetched and merged. A work ledger stops duplicated EFFORT; it does not stop
' conflicting EDITS. A council that believes otherwise will produce three
' correct, non-conflicting ledger entries and three incompatible diffs.
'
' ── WHERE IT WOULD RUN, AND THE HOLE THAT IMPLIES ──────────────────────────
' A daemon on the host is the right placement for the same reason
' `run.StateDir()` is: it is outside every agent's mount, so no agent can
' rewrite the shared record.
'
' But then each sandbox has to REACH it, and that is a deliberate hole:
'   docker backend   `host.docker.internal` is pointed at 127.0.0.1 with the
'                    host-reach add-on OFF and at `host-gateway` with it ON —
'                    the switch already exists and defaults to closed
'   sbx backend      the daemon's host:port must be added to
'                    `permissions.network.allow`, and published with
'                    `sbx ports --publish` if it is to be reached at all
' Neither is free: a council ledger is the first thing to argue for opening an
' inbound path from every sandbox to a host service, and that argument should be
' made explicitly rather than arriving as a dependency.
'
' ── THE SHARED-STATE HAZARD IS ALREADY CATALOGUED HERE ─────────────────────
' A host daemon shared by every run, outliving every run, is the SAME SHAPE as
' sbx's secret store — and credential-path.puml already wrote down what that
' costs: "a stale entry from an earlier run can therefore authenticate a later
' one that never chose it". Substitute "inform" for "authenticate" and the
' hazard transfers intact: a council reading a ledger cannot tell a current
' claim from an abandoned one unless entries carry a lease or an expiry.
'
' So the provenance work is not optional decoration on this. The four classes
' operator-fingerprint.puml proposes — human-verified, human-present,
' agent-cert, agent-asserted — are what let a council weigh an entry at all.
' Without them every entry reads as equally authoritative, which is how a
' confident wrong claim propagates through three runs instead of one.
'
' ── DECIDED: THIS LEDGER AND THE ATTESTATION LEDGER STAY SEPARATE ──────────
' Left open in both files, and now closed. They want the same mechanism —
' append-only, hash-chained, per-writer identity — and merging them would be
' tidy. It would also make a council's coordination store the same artifact as
' the provenance record for credentials, and the two have very different blast
' radii:
'   THIS ONE      written constantly, by every run, and read for scheduling
'   THE OTHER     written rarely, by a human with a touch, and read when trust
'                 is in question
' One artifact means one compromise reaches both, and one retention policy has
' to serve both. Separate, cross-referenced, and no longer an open question in
' either file. See _spec/_experiments/operator-fingerprint.puml.
'
' ── EXPERIMENTS — NOTHING BELOW IS MEASURED ─────────────────────────────────
' G1  DOES IT OFFER A LINEARISATION PRIMITIVE?
'     Compare-and-set, lease, unique constraint, or append-if-head-unchanged.
'     If not, MentisDB is an audit trail for the council and not its
'     coordinator, and the claiming problem needs a different answer. This gates
'     every other question here, so it is first and it is cheap: read the API.
'
' G2  WHAT DOES A SECOND WRITER ACTUALLY SEE, AND WHEN?
'     Two `proveo run` instances appending concurrently: does B observe A's
'     entry before acting, and what is the visibility delay? A council that
'     coordinates through a store with lazy reads will double-claim in the gap
'     regardless of what the API promises.
'
' G3  WHOSE KEY SIGNS AN ENTRY, AND CAN A RUN FORGE ANOTHER'S?
'     The agent registry holds per-chain signing keys. If a run can write under
'     another run's identity, the ledger's attribution is decorative. Probe it
'     adversarially — that is the only way this question gets a real answer.
'
' G4  WHAT DOES REACHABILITY COST?
'     Measure the actual opening: the add-host switch on docker, the allow entry
'     and published port on sbx. Then ask whether a UNIX socket bind-mounted
'     read-write into each sandbox would do the same job without an inbound
'     network path — a smaller hole for the same coordination.
'
' ── WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DECIDE ────────────────────────────
' WHETHER A COUNCIL IS WANTED AT ALL. Several concurrent runs is a capability
' decision with costs — host memory, provider spend, merge conflicts — that have
' nothing to do with ledgers. This file assumes the council and asks only what
' would coordinate it.
'
' WHETHER TO ADOPT MentisDB. It is a candidate, read from its README and not
' run. G1 decides whether it fits the coordination job; nothing here has
' measured its behaviour, its operational cost, or what depending on a
' third-party daemon does to proveo's install story.
'
' WHETHER THE LEDGER AND THE ATTESTATION LEDGER ARE ONE THING. They want the
' same properties — append-only, chained, per-writer identity — and merging them
' would be tidy. It would also make a council's coordination store the same
' artifact as the provenance record for secrets, which couples two very
' different blast radii. Named here so the tidiness is a decision.
title EXPERIMENT — an agent council needs a place to see each other;\na hash chain is not a lock

package "the host" as HOST <<host>> {
  database "**work ledger**\nappend-only · hash-chained\noutside every agent's mount" as LEDGER <<db>>
  component "proveo\n""sbx.RunningNames()"" already\nenumerates the council" as PV <<app>>
  component "the reach switch\n""host.docker.internal"" · allow entry\n· published port" as REACH <<error>>
}

package "run A" as A <<host>> {
  component "agent A\nown sid, own clone" as AA <<app>>
}
package "run B" as B <<host>> {
  component "agent B\nown sid, own clone" as AB <<app>>
}

component "**the double claim**\ntwo valid, signed, chained\nentries for one task" as DUP <<error>>
component "conflicting diffs\nthe ledger never saw them" as DIFF <<error>>

AA ARROW_MAIN LEDGER : appends a claim
AB ARROW_MAIN LEDGER : appends a claim
LEDGER ARROW_ERROR DUP : append-only cannot refuse\nthe second one
AA ARROW_ERROR DIFF
AB ARROW_ERROR DIFF : separate clones, separate\ncommits, one merge
AA ARROW_CLOUD REACH
REACH ARROW_MAIN LEDGER : a hole that defaults to closed
PV ARROW_OPTIONAL LEDGER : could write the run's own\nposture and evidence digests

note bottom of LEDGER
  **A chain proves order, not honesty.** It
  closes the "edited afterwards" half that a
  transcript in a writable mount cannot. It
  does not make an agent's account of itself
  true, and must not be sold that way.
end note

note bottom of DUP
  **G1 is the whole experiment.** Two runs can
  each append ""I claim X"" and both entries are
  valid, signed and chained. Coordination needs
  a compare-and-set or a lease; a log alone
  cannot refuse the second writer.
end note

note bottom of REACH
  **The first real argument for an inbound
  path.** The docker switch already defaults to
  closed, and sbx needs an allow entry plus a
  published port. A bind-mounted socket may do
  the same job with a smaller hole.
end note

note bottom of DIFF
  **It coordinates intent, not merges.** Clone
  mode keeps two runs from corrupting one tree,
  and leaves their commits to be fetched and
  merged. Three tidy entries can still describe
  three incompatible diffs.
end note

note right of AB
  **A stale claim reads like a live one.** Same
  shape as the secret store: an entry outliving
  the run that wrote it informs a later council
  that never chose it, unless entries carry a
  lease or an expiry.
end note

legend bottom
  **What this file settles, and what it only frames**
  ==
  **The shape is right, and better than expected**: MentisDB is append-only and
  SHA-256 hash-chained, carries an agent registry with per-chain Ed25519 signing keys,
  answers ""as_of"" point-in-time queries and exposes MCP — which is most of what
  operator-fingerprint.puml specified for a ledger, by hand, before knowing of it.
  --
  **And it does not clear the wall.** A chain proves an entry was not altered or
  reordered after writing; it says nothing about whether the entry was true. With the
  agent holding the key, the council's record is tamper-evident testimony, not evidence.
  ==
  **G1 gates everything**: an append-only log is NOT a lock. Two runs can both validly
  claim one task. Unless the store offers compare-and-set, a lease or an
  append-if-head-unchanged, it is the council's audit trail and not its coordinator —
  useful, but a different claim than the one being made for it.
  --
  **Not decided**: whether a council is wanted at all; whether to adopt MentisDB, which
  has been read and not run; and whether this ledger and the attestation ledger should be
  one artifact — tidy, and it would couple two very different blast radii.
end legend
@enduml
